        "display_name": null,
        "comment": "Static comparison and configuration analysis, 2026-10-25 UTC. This fresh 1,288,875-byte x86-64 ELF and sibling 90950ae9570d317ba26d8a72f45c3d64821d4d2c8a916e78e01c24b6b13368dd have identical ELF bytes through offset 0x13a9b0 (1,288,624 bytes), SHA-256 of that prefix 86fd6c718c91d569e9bd5e15f36b48f16bd5939538209225373121ecb03aae11. All 199 differing bytes are inside the appended 251-byte JSON trailer. Both trailers parse as config/id/enckey/ParentId; identifier and encryption-field values are deliberately omitted. These are distinct full-file hashes with the same executable code and data, rather than evidence of two code variants. No stripped or reconstructed executable was written. Parser 0x416f4b searches for the config JSON prefix; 0x41702d constructs the config marker and 0x41730b constructs the enckey field marker. Compiled primary HTTP C2 URL http://177.221.180.26/cgi-bin/p.cgi is at file offset 0x136140 (VA 0x537140). Default-selection code 0x41a0a5-0x41a0c2 copies that URL into the working endpoint buffer; check-in code at 0x41ab03-0x41ab30 formats the p.cgi request and reaches the HTTP routine. Other literals include dummy.zero (placeholder), a legacy 16-character onion hostname and an I2P address; these are not claimed as usable or active infrastructure. The JSON trailer and p.cgi protocol are consistent with Prometei, supporting the Prometei tag independently of the upstream Mirai signature. URLhaus already associates this exact file hash with a recent k.php delivery record, so no duplicate URL submission was made. No execution, C2/payload-server contact or current live-activity claim. Background comparison: https://unit42.paloaltonetworks.com/prometei-botnet-2025-activity/ ."
      }
